  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 309 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will remain anchored across the Four Corners \nregion the through the first of next week. This will allow for some \nmonsoonal-subtropical moisture to rotate north and then eastward \nacross our area around the ridge periphery. Monday into Tuesday \nshould remain mostly dry and continued quite warm, with highs well \nin the 90s at most locations. \n\nThe upper level ridging flattens some with the center translating \ninto the southern plains my the middle and latter parts of next \nweek. Ensemble guidance suggests somewhat stronger winds aloft will \ndevelop southward from the northern plains as the ridge flattens. At \nthe same time, mean PWAT values will be on the increase as the \nmonsoonal moisture increases. The ENS suggests values nearing 1.5\" \nby Wednesday night into Thursday. Several embedded shortwaves are \nexpected within the westerly flow aloft on the northern periphery \nof the southern plains upper high. The result will be increasing \nchances for showers and thunderstorms Wednesday and beyond.  \n\nA considerable  cool down also looks to be on tap by the end of next \nweek. A Canadian airmass settles southward into the northern and \ncentral plains brining relief from the hot temperatures. In fact, \ncurrent forecast guidance suggests highs may struggle out of the \nupper 60s by Friday.",
